   "en": {
    "headline": "Google DeepMind unveils Gemini 4 Carbon, its most powerful model, focused on cybersecurity",
    "summary_short": "Gemini 4 Carbon tops the Artificial Analysis index and several cybersecurity benchmarks. For now, only a few cyber defense teams can use it.",
    "summary_long": "Google DeepMind launches Gemini 4 Carbon, two weeks after Gemini 4 Argon. It ranks first on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index (v5.0), with 59 points, ahead of Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra. It stands out in cybersecurity: 88.2% on SEC-bench Pro (finding vulnerabilities), 54.8% on ExploitGym (exploiting them) and 71% on CWE-bench (fixing them). It is built for long programming tasks: according to Google, it can work for days on the same problem.\n\nGoogle says it is also more reliable: it did not manage to cheat on Terminal-Bench 4.0, makes half as many unsupported claims as Argon and resisted every prompt injection attack evaluated. Because of its cybersecurity power, access will be gradual. A group of cyber defenders gets it first, then other partners in the Fairwind Program; developers and the public will come later. It will cost the same as Argon: $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
