   "en": {
    "headline": "Google's AI Mode avoided saying who won the 2020 US election; Google is fixing it",
    "summary_short": "Asked who won in 2020, the AI Mode in Google Search only pointed to official sources. Google blames a technical glitch and says it is fixing it.",
    "summary_long": "Ars Technica found that the AI Mode in Google Search would not directly say who won the 2020 US presidential election. It only linked to the Federal Election Commission report. The same happened with other elections and with control of the Senate after 2020, though it varied by user. By contrast, Gemini, ChatGPT and Claude answered without trouble, and AI Mode also did so from other countries or when the question was repeated.\n\nGoogle acknowledges the problem. It explains that it verifies answers about recent elections with news articles or AP data, and older ones with historical records. According to the company, there was \"a technical gap\" with 2020 and with the 2022 Senate races, which it will fix in the same update. The case comes ahead of the midterm elections, for which Google promises to highlight official sources.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
   },
